To force a text field to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you'll need to use a combination of HTML and CSS. However, since you're looking for a JavaScript solution, we'll focus on using the `contenteditable` attribute and CSS grid layout.

**Step 1: Create a Container Element**

First, create a container element to hold the text field and its columns. You can use a `div` element for this purpose.

```html
<div id="container">
  <div id="text-field" contenteditable="true"></div>
</div>
```

**Step 2: Add CSS Styles**

Next, add CSS styles to create a grid layout for the text field. You can use the `display: grid` property to create a grid container.

```css
#container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); /* Create 3 equal columns */
  grid-gap: 10px; /* Add a gap between columns */
  width: 500px; /* Set the width of the container */
  height: 200px; /* Set the height of the container */
}

#text-field {
  grid-column: 1 / -1; /* Span all columns */
  padding: 10px; /* Add some padding */
  border: 1px solid #ccc; /* Add a border */
}
```

**Step 3: Add JavaScript**

Now, you can use JavaScript to create a function that splits the text into columns. You'll need to use the `split()` method to split the text into an array of words, and then use the `forEach()` method to create a new `div` element for each word.

```javascript
function splitTextIntoColumns(text) {
  const words = text.split(' ');
  const container = document.getElementById('container');
  const textField = document.getElementById('text-field');
  const columns = Array.from({ length: 3 }, () => document.createElement('div'));

  words.forEach((word, index) => {
    const column = columns[index % 3];
    const wordElement = document.createElement('span');
    wordElement.textContent = word;
    column.appendChild(wordElement);
  });

  container.appendChild(columns[0]);
  container.appendChild(columns[1]);
  container.appendChild(columns[2]);
}

// Call the function to split the text into columns
splitTextIntoColumns('This is a sample text that will be split into columns');
```

This code will create a text field with three columns, and split the text into those columns. Note that this is a basic example, and you may need to adjust the CSS and JavaScript code to fit your specific requirements.

